    {
      "query": "queries.scm",
      "capabilities": [
        "references",
        "calls"
      ],
      "reason": "A call and a selector are recorded under the name they are spelled with. Go picks the thing that name reaches from the receiver's static type, from a field promoted out of an embedded struct, and from interface dispatch, none of which the Pack has, so a member mention names a candidate and not a target."
    },
    {
      "query": "queries.scm",
      "capabilities": [
        "definitions",
        "scopes"
      ],
      "reason": "Names introduced inside a function body by `:=`, by a `range` clause or by a type switch guard are not declared, and a function literal is given no region because it has no name to give one; what a package declares is stated, what a single body declares is not."
    },
    {
      "query": "queries.scm",
      "capabilities": [
        "references",
        "definitions"
      ],
      "reason": "Interface satisfaction in Go is structural and never written down: a type implements an interface by having its methods. The only implements edges here are the two places a type is named explicitly, an embedded field in a struct and an embedded interface in an interface."
    },
    {
      "query": "queries.scm",
      "capabilities": [
        "imports"
      ],
      "reason": "An import is recorded by its quoted path and by its alias. Which module that path resolves to, and what `replace`, `exclude` and workspace directives in go.mod do to it, are build facts outside the file."
    },
    {
      "query": "queries.scm",
      "capabilities": [
        "definitions"
      ],
      "reason": "Whether a Go name is exported is the case of its first letter and nothing in the syntax states it, so declarations here carry no visibility and a query cannot filter them by it."
    },
    {
      "query": "queries.scm",
      "capabilities": [
        "definitions",
        "references"
      ],
      "reason": "Build constraints are comments and file-name suffixes, so every file is read as if it were built; a declaration behind `//go:build` appears here even on a platform that would never compile it."
    },
    {
      "query": "queries.scm",
      "capabilities": [
        "tests"
      ],
      "reason": "A test is recognised by Go's own naming convention, a top-level function whose name begins Test, Benchmark, Fuzz or Example. Whether it lives in a _test.go file, and which package code it exercises, are not stated."
    }
